lanwatch (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * UniFi controller ([unifi], read-only, API key): per client the uplink
    access point or switch, wired/wireless, guest access, the controller's
    name and the network/VLAN; the networks, SSIDs and UniFi devices with
    state, ports, radios and pending firmware. New rules
    net.iot-on-main-network, inventory.unifi-only and
    net.unifi-device-offline; the flat-network note groups by the
    controller's networks. A Topology page draws gateway, UniFi devices and
    clients as a tree, with the same tree as a list. "lanwatch unifi" reads
    the controller on demand. See /etc/lanwatch/config.toml.default and
    /usr/share/doc/lanwatch/unifi.md.
  * /etc/lanwatch/config.toml is now a comment-only stub that will not
    change between versions, so upgrades stop asking about it. The full
    reference with every setting lives next to it as config.toml.default,
    refreshed on each upgrade, and the upgrade lists settings that are new
    since the previous version. This upgrade asks once more about the old
    file; answer N to keep yours.
  * "lanwatch config show" prints *** for a set API key, token or password.
  * Firmware findings read "has an update available" when the latest
    version is not known, as for UniFi devices.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 15:02:35 +0100

lanwatch (0.5.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Discovery no longer fails when [latency] targets names an address
    outside the local networks (such as 1.1.1.1): the helper refused the
    ping and the error took the whole round down. Pings now run from the
    service itself, which needs no privilege, and a target that cannot be
    measured is logged and skipped.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:33:15 +0100

lanwatch (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Latency: a few pings to the gateway every discovery round. The dashboard
    shows the round-trip time with a day's trend, and sustained packet loss
    becomes a finding (net.packet-loss). Add an internet address under
    [latency] targets to measure the path out as well.
  * Internet exposure (opt-in, [exposure]): a daily lookup of the public
    address in Shodan's InternetDB. Ports that answer from the internet are
    a finding, critical when Shodan matched the service to published
    vulnerabilities. Shown on the infrastructure page; "lanwatch exposure"
    runs it by hand. Off by default: it tells Shodan the public address.
  * Presence: every device page shows a heatmap of the hours and weekdays
    the device answered over the last four weeks, and names the hour it is
    most often away.
  * Randomised MAC addresses: a new device with a private (locally
    administered) address is reported at low severity, and a note explains
    why it may reappear as a new device. A prefix that no manufacturer
    holds in the IEEE registry is a separate low finding.
  * Home Assistant: with notify.home_assistant.lanwatch_url set, tapping a
    notification opens lanwatch, and a single new finding gets an "Accept"
    button ("Yes, that's mine" for a new device). docs/home-assistant.md,
    installed with the package, covers the token, the sensors, the buttons
    and troubleshooting.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:11:09 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Devices the scanner could not name now get their vendor from the IEEE
    registry: arp-scan ships an OUI list from March 2024, so every prefix
    assigned since (e4:65:b8, Espressif, among many) showed no vendor. The
    registry is downloaded weekly by a new "oui" job; with [oui] online =
    false, nmap's installed copy is used instead.
  * Firmware and OUI online lookups are on by default. Both fetch public
    lists and send nothing about the network; online = false turns them off.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 10:32:13 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * LLM: when an answer is not valid JSON, the log now says why the model
    stopped (done_reason), how many tokens went in and out, and how the
    answer ended. A reply cut off mid-way can then be told from a wrong one.
  * Suggestions are made weekly by `lanwatch run`, and the status page has a
    "Run now" button for them. Before, only `lanwatch suggest` made them.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 11:52:41 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Service scans no longer fail with "ValueError: Separator is found, but
    chunk is longer than limit". The result of a scan comes back from the
    helper as one line, and lanwatch read it with a 64 KiB limit that a scan
    of more than a few devices exceeds.
  * Web UI: the edit form on a device's page kept its fields outside the box
    on anything wider than a phone. They are back inside.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 23:25:40 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Service scans run in batches with a realistic time limit each. A single
    run over all devices exceeded its 15 minute limit on networks of a few
    dozen devices, and the whole inventory was lost every time.
  * lanwatch no longer reports its own decoy ports as open services on the
    machine it runs on, nor its own addresses as decoy visitors.
  * The default /etc/lanwatch/config.toml mentions [scan], [firmware] and
    [decoy]. This changes a conffile: keep your own version when asked.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 22:56:32 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Decoy ports ([decoy]): a connection to a port nothing uses becomes a
    finding. Ports already in use are skipped. The service gains
    CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for the ports below 1024; note that a port listed
    under [decoy] is not available to a service installed later.
  * Passive ARP listener in the helper: spoofing between scans, and devices
    sweeping the network.
  * Decoy name lookups over LLMNR, mDNS and NetBIOS detect poisoning tools.
  * New IPv6 routers are reported.
  * Firmware check ([firmware], daily, "lanwatch firmware") for Home
    Assistant, ESPHome, Shelly, Tasmota, Pi-hole and Jellyfin. Looking up the
    latest release online is off by default.
  * Optional check of whether the DHCP-announced DNS resolver filters ads or
    malicious domains (scan.dns_filter_check, off by default).

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 16:03:21 +0100

lanwatch (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stopping or restarting no longer waits for the scan in progress; the scan
    is cancelled, also inside the helper.
  * A configuration error leaves the service failed with the reason in the
    journal instead of restarting it every ten seconds.
  * Web UI: Status page (jobs, "Run now", scan privileges, LLM), a scan
    indicator, and protection against cross-site form posts.
  * Devices can be re-classified, merged, forgotten and watched; private MAC
    addresses are marked. Findings can be accepted with a reason and reopened.
  * New rules: IP conflict, changed MAC on the gateway/DHCP/DNS address,
    changed DHCP-announced settings, watched device offline.
  * The helper only runs lanwatch's own nmap scripts against networks this
    machine is attached to. Extend with LANWATCH_HELPER_ALLOW_SCRIPTS and
    LANWATCH_HELPER_ALLOW_NETS (see README.Debian).
  * History is pruned daily ([store] keep_*_days).
  * --verbose on the commands that take time.
  * Concurrent writers (service, CLI, web UI) wait for each other instead of
    failing with "database is locked".
  * arm64 package.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 15:11:04 +0100

